<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Mysql on jpcercal.com</title><link>https://staging.jpcercal.com/pr-125/en/tags/mysql/</link><description>Recent content in Mysql on jpcercal.com</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Mon, 21 Oct 2013 12:10:18 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://staging.jpcercal.com/pr-125/en/tags/mysql/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>MySQLDump - MySQL database backup</title><link>https://staging.jpcercal.com/pr-125/en/mysqldump-mysql-database-backup/</link><pubDate>Mon, 21 Oct 2013 12:10:18 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://staging.jpcercal.com/pr-125/en/mysqldump-mysql-database-backup/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Using the &lt;strong&gt;mysqldump&lt;/strong&gt; tool, you can back up a local database and restore it to a&#10;remote database with the same characteristics, all using a simple command. Next, you&amp;rsquo;ll see&#10;practical examples of using the &lt;strong&gt;mysqldump&lt;/strong&gt; tool to export and import&#10;&lt;em&gt;MySQL&lt;/em&gt; databases.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Below is an export example:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;&#10;&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;&#10;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;&#10;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;$ mysqldump -u &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;USERNAME&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; -p&lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;PASSWORD&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;DATABASE-NAME&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &amp;gt; dumpfilename.sql&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;And to import the database:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;&#10;&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;&#10;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;1&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;&#10;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-shell" data-lang="shell"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;$ mysql -u &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;USERNAME&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; -p&lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;PASSWORD&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;DATABASE-NAME&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &amp;lt; dumpfilename.sql&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Based on the syntax shown above, let&amp;rsquo;s say there is a database called &amp;ldquo;exemplo&amp;rdquo;, and we want to export it.&#10;To do this, we would run the following command:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>